# prompts and error messages
1.1:Invalid number of parameters
1.2:Invalid date
1.3:Invalid switch
1.4:Source path too long
1.5:Invalid source drive specification
1.6:Source path not found
1.7:Source path too long
1.8:Destination path too long
1.9:Invalid destination drive specification
1.10:Does
1.11:specify a file name
1.12:or directory name on the target
1.13:File
1.14:Directory
1.15:Destination path too long
1.16:Cannot perform a cyclic copy
1.17:Press enter to continue...
1.18:File not found

# the help message
2.1:Copies files and directory trees.
2.2:XCOPY source [destination] [/switches]
2.3:  source       Specifies the directory and/or name of file(s) to copy.
2.4:  destination  Specifies the location and/or name of new file(s).
2.5:  /A           Copies only files with the archive attribute set and doesn't
2.6:               change the attribute.
2.7:  /C           Continues copying even if errors occur.
2.8:  /D[:M/D/Y]   Copies only files which have been changed on or after the
2.9:               specified date. When no date is specified, only files which are
2.10:               newer than existing destination files will be copied.
2.11:  /E           Copies any subdirectories, even if empty.
2.12:  /F           Display full source and destination name.
2.13:  /H           Copies hidden and system files as well as unprotected files.
2.14:  /I           If destination does not exist and copying more than one file,
2.15:               assume destination is a directory.
2.16:  /L           List files without copying them. (simulates copying)
2.17:  /M           Copies only files with the archive attribute set and turns off
2.18:               the archive attribute of the source files after copying them.
2.19:  /N           Suppresses prompting to confirm you want to overwrite an
2.20:               existing destination file and skips these files.
2.21:  /P           Prompts for confirmation before creating each destination file.
2.22:  /Q           Quiet mode, don't show copied filenames.
2.23:  /R           Overwrite read-only files as well as unprotected files.
2.24:  /S           Copies directories and subdirectories except empty ones.
2.25:  /T           Creates directory tree without copying files. Empty directories
2.26:               will not be copied. To copy them add switch /E.
2.27:  /V           Verifies each new file.
2.28:  /W           Waits for a keypress before beginning.
2.29:  /Y           Suppresses prompting to confirm you want to overwrite an
2.30:               existing destination file and overwrites these files.
2.31:  /-Y          Causes prompting to confirm you want to overwrite an existing
2.32:               destination file.
2.33:The switch /Y or /N may be preset in the COPYCMD environment variable.
2.34:This may be overridden with /-Y on the command line.
2.35:press enter for more

